---
import SightlineLayout from "@/layouts/SightlineLayout.astro";
---

<SightlineLayout
  title="Methodology — Sightline"
  description="How Sightline bounds visual search, solves an absolute camera, verifies rendered evidence, and protects private metadata."
>
  <div class="sightline-shell">
    <header class="sightline-page-head">
      <p class="sightline-kicker">Method and limits</p>
      <h1>Evidence before appearance.</h1>
      <p class="sightline-lede">
        Sightline separates a visually plausible hypothesis from a publishable
        camera. Every stage can stop without erasing the photograph or
        disguising the failure.
      </p>
    </header>
    <section class="method-copy">
      <h2>Metadata-assisted</h2><div>
        <p>
          Reviewed exact GPS narrows the search but is never the final camera.
          Street-level references, local feature matching, provider depth, and
          robust PnP recover a metric pose. When position evidence is stronger
          than the absolute solve, Sightline may hold corrected GPS fixed and
          solve orientation only.
        </p><p>
          Mean-sea-level altitude is corrected to the WGS84 ellipsoid before
          comparison. Device direction and gravity may guide the search, but
          verification decides the result.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="method-copy">
      <h2>Image-only</h2><div>
        <p>
          The <code>sf-north-v1</code> experiment receives metadata-stripped pixels,
          an opaque identifier, and fixed geographic bounds. It cannot read GPS, heading,
          focal length, device orientation, timestamp, source filename, or private
          metadata.
        </p><p>
          Predictions are sealed before a separate evaluator may compare them
          with embedded device metadata. Those differences are diagnostic, not
          survey truth.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="method-copy">
      <h2>Verification</h2><div>
        <ol>
          <li>Render the solved camera at the source aspect ratio.</li><li>
            Match the source photograph against that exact render.
          </li><li>
            Measure coverage, residual structure, depth consistency, and
            displacement.
          </li><li>Reject broad disagreement or insufficient evidence.</li><li>
            Re-render any repaired pose and apply the same tests again.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="method-copy">
      <h2>Publication boundary</h2><div>
        <p>
          A verified camera does not automatically authorize projective texture.
          Exact location, comparison tools, and projection remain separate
          editorial decisions. Rounded, city-level, and hidden records cannot
          leak exact camera geometry.
        </p><p>
          Provider imagery, identifiers, rich EXIF, credentials, local paths,
          and detailed matcher evidence remain outside the public artifact.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="method-copy">
      <h2>Limits</h2><div>
        <p>
          Sightline is not survey-grade ground truth. GPS uncertainty, scene
          changes, simplified lens assumptions, panorama pose, provider
          photogrammetry, occlusion, and incomplete depth all affect the result.
          Confidence ranks evidence; it never replaces the exact-render audit.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</SightlineLayout>
